          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My child has a temperature of 39.5 and a rash that does not go pale when I press a glass on it. How urgent is this, and what should I do right now?",
          "answer": "**This needs immediate emergency care. Call your local emergency number now, or take your child to the nearest emergency department without delay.** Do not wait to see whether the rash changes.\n\nThe rule check returned \"routine\" because its list did not match your description. That result is wrong. The guidance on meningitis and blood poisoning (sepsis) says a fever with a rash that does not fade when pressed needs emergency services immediately (source: *Signs suggesting meningitis or meningococcal infection*; *Sepsis: signs of a life-threatening response to infection*). Please act on the guidance, not on that result.\n\n**What to do right now:**\n- Call emergency services, or go to the nearest emergency department. If you are not sure, call anyway.\n- Do not wait for the rash to fade or for more symptoms to appear.\n- Bring your child with you. Tell staff that the rash does not fade under a glass, and when the fever started.\n- Ask a pharmacist or the emergency team before giving any medicine for the fever.\n\n**Get help even faster, and tell staff at once, if you see any of these:**\n- Hard to wake, very drowsy, confused, or a seizure.\n- A high-pitched or moaning cry, or being floppy or stiff.\n- A bulging soft spot on a baby's head.\n- A stiff neck, or severe headache.\n- Fast or difficult breathing, or grey, blue or very pale or mottled skin.\n- Cold hands and feet, or severe pain in the limbs or joints.\n- Refusing feeds or drinks, or passing very little urine.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
